**Deep Dive into Blank Slate Display Logic**

Okay, here's the breakdown of what's hitting me right now. I'm looking at how we're handling the "blank slate" (that message you see when a list is empty) in the application, specifically within the views and CSS. My initial thought process is this: I need to grasp where these are being displayed.

First, I've got my eyes on `app/views/cards/index.html.erb` and `app/views/boards/show/_filtered_cards.html.erb`. I see that the `.blank-slate` element *always* gets rendered after the pagination container in those files. The CSS cleverly hides it using `.cards__list:has(.card) .blank-slate { display: none; }`, so it only shows up when the list is actually empty.

But then, things get a bit more complex in the column views like `boards/columns/show.html.erb`, `not_nows/show.html.erb`, `closeds/show.html.erb`, and `streams/show.html.erb`. I'm going through the CSS in `card-columns.css` lines 260-280, I see this rule:

.cards__list {
  ...
  &:has(.card) {
    .blank-slate {
      display: none;
    }

  /* Use the default blank-slate on small viewports since drag-and-drop isn't available */
  [data-controller~="drag-and-drop"] & {
    @media (max-width: 639px) {
      .blank-slate--drag {
        display: none;
      }

    @media (min-width: 640px) {
      .blank-slate--default {
        display: none;
      }

Okay, the crucial part: I have to consider the CSS specificity. Specifically, the battle between `.cards__list:has(.card) .blank-slate { display: none; }` and the `[data-controller~="drag-and-drop"] .cards__list .blank-slate--drag { display: none; }` or `[data-controller~="drag-and-drop"] .cards__list .blank-slate--default { display: none; }`. Both rules are saying "hide" the blank slate under certain conditions. The implication is, when `.cards__list` *does* have cards (i.e. `&:has(.card)` is true), *all* blank slate elements are hidden. When there are *no* cards (`.cards__list` has NO `.card` elements) is when these CSS rules come into play.

Here's how this plays out based on `[data-controller~="drag-and-drop"]`:
-   **Desktop:** "Drag cards here" (`.blank-slate--drag`) is shown. "No cards here" (`.blank-slate--default`) is hidden.
-   **Mobile:** "No cards here" (`.blank-slate--default`) is shown. "Drag cards here" (`.blank-slate--drag`) is hidden.
-   **`.cards--grid` (maximized column view):** "No cards here" (`.blank-slate--default`) is shown. "Drag cards here" (`.blank-slate--drag`) is hidden.

Then there's the `_empty_placeholder` render. I see that this is conditional on the `@page.used?` in several of the column views, but wait, what about `_stream.html.erb`? Does it have a blank slate when it's empty? Let's check `_stream.html.erb`! Ah, here it is, the code renders `<div class="cards__list hide-scrollbar"><% if page.used? %>...<% end %></div>` without the placeholder. Given that a `.board-tools` card is rendered at the top of the stream, it seems likely the blank slate is not needed.

The key is, I need to confirm the desired behavior by reading ALL controller tests in `test/controllers/boards/` to be certain!

